  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Express the normal gradient using the contact input",
      "body": "<p>The curve has derivative <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, so its tangent gradient at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>. The normal gradient is its negative reciprocal, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "The normal is perpendicular to the tangent at the same contact point.",
      "pitfall": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> would give a vertical normal, which cannot be the finite-slope line in the question.",
      "pause": "Use the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to write the normal equation.",
      "recall": [
